<resources xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">
    <!--
      The frame before the webview has painted anything.

      Not the status or navigation bar: `MainActivity` calls enableEdgeToEdge(),
      so those are transparent and the page itself shows through them — which is
      what the scroll fade at the top of the app is drawn against, and tinting
      them here would put an opaque band back over it.

      What is left is the window's own background, which the system paints for
      the moment between the activity starting and the first frame of the app.
      Left alone that is Material's flat white, so a launch into the app's dark
      palette is a white flash. Android 12 and up can do better: the same
      wallpaper-derived neutral the app is about to paint itself in.

      Approximate on purpose. It follows the *system's* light/dark setting, not
      the app's, because that is all a resource qualifier can see — someone
      running the app light on a dark phone still gets one dark frame. Matching
      exactly would mean reading the app's own stored appearance from native
      code before the webview exists, which is a lot of machinery for one frame.
    -->
    <style name="Theme.app" parent="Theme.MaterialComponents.DayNight.NoActionBar">
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@android:color/system_neutral1_10</item>
    </style>
</resources>
